'''''Melinis minutiflora''''', commonly known as '''molasses grass''', is a species of [[Poaceae|grass]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url = http://www.fs.fed.us/database/feis/plants/graminoid/melmin/all.html#DISTRIBUTIONANDOCCURRENCE|title = Melinis minutiflora|date = 2008|accessdate = 24 November 2015|website = US Department of Agriculture Forest Service|publisher = U.S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service|last = Hauser|first = A. Scott}}</ref>

It is a perennial grass native to [[Africa]], where it occurs in disjunct populations (an arc from central [[Angola]] to [[Cameroon]] in western central africa, the areas around [[Ruwenzori Mountains]] and [[Mount Kenya]] in eastern Africa.<ref name=":0" /> Its seeds are dispersed by wind.<ref name=":0" /> Molasses grass usually grows to be thirty to sixty inches tall, and it forms mats when its long, slender stems lay on top of each other in layers up to four feet deep.<ref name=":0" /> Molasses grass can spread up other plants, using them as support, like a vine.<ref name=":0" /> It has fragrant foliage and colorful inflorescences.<ref name=":0" /> It blooms for short periods with differing bloom times depending on where the grass is located.<ref name=":1">{{cite web|title=Melinis minutiflora|url=http://www.tropicalforages.info/key/Forages/Media/Html/Melinis_minutiflora.htm|website=Tropical Forages|accessdate=24 November 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161106175340/http://www.tropicalforages.info/key/Forages/Media/Html/Melinis_minutiflora.htm|archive-date=6 November 2016|url-status=dead}}</ref> Molasses grass generally flowers in the [[Southern Hemisphere|southern hemisphere]] between April and June and in the [[Northern Hemisphere|northern hemisphere]] in November.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":1" />

==Role as a non-native species==
It was introduced to tropical areas such as [[Hawaii]] for livestock feed, and is now naturalized in some areas.<ref name=":0" /> Around 1812, molasses grass took root in [[Brazil]] and spread through abandoned coffee plantations.<ref name=":0" /> Similarly, around 1900, molasses grass was introduced in Hawaii for cattle grazing.<ref name=":0" /> In Hawaii, it continued to spread, though its spread was hindered by feral goats.<ref name=":0" /> It is considered an invasive species in many parts of the world, including some Pacific Islands and South America.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Hoffman|first1=William A.|last2=Haridasan|first2=M|title=The invasive grass, Melinis minutiflora, inhibits tree regeneration in a neo-tropical savannah|journal=Austral Ecology|date=2008|volume=2008|pages=29–36|doi=10.1111/j.1442-9993.2007.01787.x|citeseerx=10.1.1.492.1957}}</ref>

Molasses grass plays a large role in [[fire ecology]] in the regions where it grows.<ref name=":0" /> In the Brazilian [[savanna]] (''[[Cerrado]]'') this species is invasive and dominant due to the increased frequency of fire.<ref>Hoffmann, W.A. & Jackson, R.B. (2000) Vegetation–climate feedbacks in the conversion of tropical savanna to grassland. Journal of Climate, 13, 1593–1602.</ref> When a wild fire occurs, molasses grass is very quick to colonize the disturbed area.<ref name=":0" /> Examples of this are seen in almost every region where molasses grass is found, including Australia, Brazil and Hawaii, where molasses grass even increases the risk of fire in the areas in which it establishes.<ref name=":0" /> Molasses grass increases the likelihood of fires where it has established because it is brittle and burns easily.<ref name=":0" /> This cycle where molasses grass establishes, burns easily, then reestablishes and burns again is known as the grass/fire cycle, and it undermines native plants' ability to grow and thrive in their native environment.<ref name=":0" /> One result of this cycle is a drop in biodiversity in the affected area.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Hoffmann|first1=William A.|last2=Lucatelli|first2=Verusca M. P. C.|last3=Silva|first3=Franciane J.|last4=Azeuedo|first4=Isaac N. C.|last5=da S. Marinho|first5=Marcelo|last6=Albuquerque|first6=Ana Maria S.|last7=de O. Lopes|first7=Apoena|last8=Moreira|first8=Silvana P.|title=Impact of the invasive alien grass Melinis minutiflora at the savannah-forest eco-tone in the Brazilian Cerrado|journal=Diversity and Distributions|date=2004|volume=10|issue=2|pages=99–103|doi=10.1111/j.1366-9516.2004.00063.x|s2cid=34511758 |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/227687525|accessdate=24 November 2015}}</ref>

